<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Kitts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint Christopher Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Kitts, formally Saint Christopher, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-warner">Thomas Warner</a> founded the first English colony in the Caribbean there in 1623, and the French settled in 1625; the two partitioned the island in 1627. It was the base for further English and French colonisation and passed wholly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1713.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-kitts.jpg" alt="Saint Kitts" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Burmesedays · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Kitts_and_Nevis_Regions_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saint Kitts lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the Lesser Antilles, with its west side on the Caribbean Sea and its east coast facing the Atlantic. It is about 168 square kilometres in area and 29 kilometres long. Nevis lies 3 kilometres to the south across a shallow channel called The Narrows. The island is named for Saint Christopher, Kit being a formerly common short form of Christopher.</p><p>It has three groups of volcanic peaks, and the highest point is the dormant volcano Mount Liamuiga, formerly Mount Misery, at about 1,156 metres. The interior is too steep for habitation, and a ring road runs round the coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the last ice age Saint Kitts, Nevis, Saba and Sint Eustatius were one island. The first settlers were Archaic people who came south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Around 100 BCE the farming Saladoid people arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a>, and around 800 CE the Arawakan-speaking Igneri replaced them. About 1300 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> dispersed the Igneri and named the island Liamuiga, fertile island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> claimed it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1493.</p><p>The first English colony was founded in 1623, followed by a French one in 1625. The colonists united to forestall a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> ambush, massacred the Kalinago, and then partitioned the island, the English taking the middle and the French each end. A Spanish force seized the island in 1629, and the English settlement was rebuilt after the peace of 1630. Through the 17th and 18th centuries the island passed repeatedly between English, later British, and French control.</p><p>Tobacco was the first crop, but farmers switched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> in 1640, and the labour-intensive cultivation led to large-scale importation of enslaved Africans. Parliament outlawed the purchase of enslaved people in 1807 and abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> with effect from 1 August 1834, followed by four years of apprenticeship, against which the nominally freed people revolted until martial law was declared. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> became independent on 19 September 1983.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> form one country, the Federation of Saint Kitts and Nevis, whose capital and largest port is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a> on Saint Kitts. English is the main language. Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, is the largest fortress ever built in the Eastern Caribbean.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Kitts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cayo-style pottery, found from Grenada to Basse-Terre and possibly <b>Saint Kitts</b> and dated between 1000 CE and 1500, resembles the mainland Koriabo style and the Meillacoid and Chicoid styles of the Greater Antilles, while Suazan Troumassoid pottery grew in the islands from earlier Saladoid traditions.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Warner landed on <b>Saint Christopher</b> (Saint Kitts) with a small group of settlers in 1623, founding the first English colony in the Caribbean.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Colony of Saint Christopher</a></li><li><blockquote>The Norman captain Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc landed on <b>Saint Christopher</b> in 1625 and, with the backing of Cardinal Richelieu's Compagnie de Saint-Christophe, founded a French colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-christophe">French Saint-Christophe</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations between <b>Saint Kitts</b> and Nevis, and dependence on passport sales, remain the standing issues of its politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a></li></ul></section>
